-- Second source-checked group. Retain original audio and script.
DO $review$
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.drugs WHERE id='b0851318-4385-4e69-b85f-12ec8af5b5cb' AND preparation='For emergency lipid rescue, use the locally stocked 20% emulsion and LAST rescue pack. Follow the product instructions and the local emergency administration protocol. The Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10 does not specify an in-line filter rule for LAST. Parenteral nutrition is a separate indication: the MHRA advises using integrated or add-on filters during PN administration. Source for the PN distinction: https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/filters-should-be-used-during-the-administration-of-parenteral-nutrition-for-patients-in-all-care-settings') THEN
    UPDATE public.drugs SET preparation='For emergency lipid rescue, use the locally stocked 20% emulsion and LAST rescue pack. Follow the product instructions and the local emergency administration protocol. The Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10 does not specify an in-line filter rule for LAST. Parenteral nutrition is a separate indication: the MHRA advises using integrated or add-on filters during PN administration. Source for the PN distinction: https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/filters-should-be-used-during-the-administration-of-parenteral-nutrition-for-patients-in-all-care-settings',updated_at=now() WHERE id='b0851318-4385-4e69-b85f-12ec8af5b5cb' AND slug='intralipid' AND preparation='Administer undiluted via a large peripheral or central vein. Do not use an in-line filter. Do not mix with other drugs/solutions.';
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Lipid preparation changed during review'; END IF;
  END IF;
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.podcasts WHERE id='62f41b22-c034-4df1-88f4-b86cf9ee1234' AND audit_hold=true AND status='failed' AND audit_hold_reason='Original recording misattributes a laparoscopic recovery result to LOLIPOP and describes the Weibel review as strong evidence of benefit. See Cochrane CD009642 and the distinct ALLEGRO trial (PMID 39602290). This recording needs a reviewed replacement.') THEN
    UPDATE public.podcasts SET audit_hold=true,status='failed',audit_hold_reason='Original recording misattributes a laparoscopic recovery result to LOLIPOP and describes the Weibel review as strong evidence of benefit. See Cochrane CD009642 and the distinct ALLEGRO trial (PMID 39602290). This recording needs a reviewed replacement.',error_message='Clinical review in progress. Use the current written pain-medicine topic and primary references.',updated_at=now() WHERE id='62f41b22-c034-4df1-88f4-b86cf9ee1234' AND topic_id='pain-medicine' AND voice='alloy' AND status='ready' AND audit_hold=false AND regenerating=false AND audio_path='pain-medicine.mp3' AND script='Hello and welcome. Pain is at the very heart of what we do as anaesthetists. Whether it''s acute postoperative pain, a complex chronic pain syndrome on your ward round, or a palliative patient in distress, a deep understanding of pain medicine is essential for your daily practice and for success in the FRCA and FFICM exams. Examiners love to test this because it reveals your ability to think through physiology, pharmacology, and holistic patient care.

Let''s start with the absolute fundamentals: the different types of pain. You need to be able to distinguish between nociceptive, neuropathic, and nociplastic pain, because this will guide your entire management plan. Nociceptive pain is the straightforward ''normal'' pain from tissue injury, like a surgical incision. Neuropathic pain arises from a lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system, think post-herpetic neuralgia or painful diabetic neuropathy. Then there’s nociplastic pain, which involves altered nociception without any clear evidence of actual tissue damage or nerve lesion. This is about central sensitisation, where the central nervous system gets wound up and amplifies pain signals. Fibromyalgia is the classic example here.

For acute, nociceptive pain, our modern approach is all about multimodal, opioid-sparing analgesia. The goal is to combine different agents that act via different mechanisms to improve pain relief while reducing opioid consumption and side effects. Your toolkit should include paracetamol and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, or NSAIDs, where not contraindicated. But we can add much more. Intravenous magnesium is a useful adjunct; a Cochrane review from 2013 showed it can reduce twenty-four-hour opioid use by about twenty-five percent. Intravenous lidocaine infusions have also been popular, with strong evidence for their benefit in open abdominal surgery according to a 2018 review by Weibel. It is worth noting, however, that the recent LOLIPOP trial in 2024 questioned its benefit in laparoscopic surgery. Other options in your multimodal plan can include gabapentinoids and low-dose ketamine infusions.

Now, let''s turn to neuropathic pain. The NICE guideline, CG173, is your go-to reference here. Imagine a patient two weeks after a thoracotomy who describes a burning, shooting pain with allodynia around their scar. This is classic neuropathic post-thoracotomy pain. Your first-line treatment should be a monotherapy, chosen from four key drugs: amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin, or pregabalin. You''d choose based on the patient''s comorbidities and potential drug interactions. You could, for example, start amitriptyline at ten milligrams at night, titrating up towards seventy-five milligrams. Or, you might choose pregabalin, starting at seventy-five milligrams twice a day and titrating upwards. It''s crucial to continue simple multimodal analgesia like paracetamol whilst you do this. For very localised areas of allodynia, a topical five percent lidocaine patch can also be very effective. If the first drug doesn''t work after four to six weeks, you should switch to a different first-line agent before thinking about combining them, and consider an early referral to the chronic pain multi-disciplinary team.

Chronic pain is a huge topic, and it''s vital to distinguish chronic primary pain from other forms. For chronic primary pain, where there is no clear underlying cause, NICE guideline NG193 from 2021 is very clear. You should NOT offer paracetamol, NSAIDs, opioids, or even gabapentinoids. This is a common exam trap. Instead, management is focused on restoring function. You should offer therapies like a structured exercise programme, psychological therapies like Cognitive Behavioural Therapy, known as CBT, or Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, known as ACT, and sometimes acupuncture. Certain antidepressants may also have a role. This non-pharmacological focus applies to conditions like fibromyalgia, which is understood as a nociplastic pain state. The evidence supports aerobic exercise, CBT and antidepressants like duloxetine or amitriptyline, while strongly advising against opioids and NSAIDs.

For Complex Regional Pain Syndrome, or CRPS, you need a structured approach. Diagnosis is made using the Budapest criteria. Management hinges on early involvement of the multi-disciplinary team, including physiotherapists, occupational therapists and psychologists. Key therapies include graded motor imagery and mirror therapy. For refractory cases, Spinal Cord Stimulation, or SCS, is a NICE-approved option. And a great pearl to mention is that for prevention after a wrist fracture, giving vitamin C five hundred milligrams daily for fifty days has been shown to be effective.

Finally, let''s tackle opioid rotation, a critical skill, especially in palliative care. Let''s use an example. A patient with cancer is on oral morphine modified-release, sixty milligrams twice a day. They start vomiting and can no longer absorb oral drugs. You need to convert them to a twenty-four-hour subcutaneous diamorphine infusion. First, calculate the total daily dose: sixty milligrams twice a day is one hundred and twenty milligrams of oral morphine. The conversion from oral morphine to subcutaneous morphine is two to one, so that''s sixty milligrams of subcutaneous morphine. The conversion from subcutaneous morphine to subcutaneous diamorphine is approximately three to one. This gives you twenty milligrams of subcutaneous diamorphine over twenty-four hours. Now for the most important step: you must reduce this dose to account for incomplete cross-tolerance. A reduction of twenty-five to fifty percent is recommended. So you would start the syringe driver at around fifteen milligrams of diamorphine over twenty-four hours, and prescribe a breakthrough dose of roughly one-sixth of the daily dose, which is about two point five milligrams of subcutaneous diamorphine. Remember, conversions involving methadone are complex and should be considered specialist-only.

So, when an examiner asks you about pain, remember to structure your answer. Think about the pain mechanism first. For chronic primary pain, remember the mantra of function and psychological support over pills. And if you’re asked to do an opioid conversion, don’t forget that crucial dose reduction for safety.

Here are your take-home pearls. First, always distinguish the pain mechanism—nociceptive, neuropathic, or nociplastic—as this guides your entire plan. Second, for chronic primary pain, remember NICE NG193: do not offer opioids, NSAIDs or gabapentinoids; focus on exercise, psychology and function. Third, for neuropathic pain, your first-line drugs according to NICE are amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin, or pregabalin. And fourth, when performing an opioid rotation, always calculate the equivalent daily dose and then apply a twenty-five to a fifty percent reduction for incomplete cross-tolerance.

Mastering these concepts will make you a safer, more effective clinician and will certainly help you shine in your exams. Good luck with your revision.';
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Pain episode changed during review'; END IF;
  END IF;
END;
$review$;
